Writes one of ADR-0100's maps of your topic as a record: A domain map, B prerequisite graph, C claim inventory, D evidence mapping, E competency map, F misconception map, G application map, H variability map, I gap audit, J version audit. Each item has a short name, unique in the map, and its text, and cites the stored page passages it rests on by page id and passage number, as fetch and search_pages show them. Code checks each cited passage exists, and that:
- in C every claim carries its claimType, scope and confidence, names its domain (an item of map A), cites passages (a claim with no evidence belongs in I as a gap), and an implementation claim gives appliesTo, the product and version it is true of;
- in D each entry is one publication (a book, a documentation set, a paper, an article), naming its sourceTier and its sourceDate (when it was published or updated, or the version it documents) and citing the pages of it you use;
- in B every item gives `after`, naming only items of the map, with no cycle;
- in I a gap names its domain when it is one;
- in J every item gives `checkBy`.
Map A is written first. A map is written once; add to it or change it with change_map. A big map can be written in parts: the first part with write_map, the rest with change_map.
